WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.074
Framing is the first parsing decision.

2
00:00:02.074 --> 00:00:10.717
Before a parser can interpret fields, it needs to know where a message starts, where it ends, and how much data is required to continue.

3
00:00:10.717 --> 00:00:12.791
Some protocols use fixed-size headers.

4
00:00:12.791 --> 00:00:18.669
Others use length-prefixed records, streaming records, nested sections, or a mixture of fixed and variable data.

5
00:00:18.669 --> 00:00:25.583
A safe parser does not assume that one file read, socket read, or device read contains exactly one complete message.

6
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:33.189
Input may arrive in pieces, multiple records may be batched together, and a partial header may need to wait for more bytes.

7
00:00:33.189 --> 00:00:36.300
Framing logic should distinguish incomplete data from invalid data.

8
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.757
Incomplete data means the parser needs more bytes within policy.

9
00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:44.597
Invalid data means the parser should reject the record and preserve a safe state.

10
00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:46.671
Length fields are untrusted until checked.

11
00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:51.511
A length value should be validated before allocation, buffering, copying, decompression, or nested parsing.

12
00:00:51.511 --> 00:00:58.771
The parser should compare the declared length against bytes actually available, maximum record size, maximum message size, and any state-specific

13
00:00:58.771 --> 00:00:59.117
policy.

14
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:04.649
It should also check derived values, such as header length plus body length, before using them.

15
00:01:04.649 --> 00:01:12.600
Good framing code tracks three questions: how many bytes are available, how many bytes are required, and whether continuing would exceed a limit.

16
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.711
That makes boundary handling boring in the best way.

17
00:01:15.711 --> 00:01:21.589
The parser either waits for more data, rejects the message, or proceeds with a clearly bounded frame.

18
00:01:21.589 --> 00:01:28.503
This also improves observability because failures can be classified as truncated input, invalid length, unsupported record type, or resource limit

19
00:01:28.503 --> 00:01:32.651
reached instead of one vague parse error during troubleshooting and support escalation.

20
00:01:32.651 --> 00:01:36.800
That precision helps teams fix the right layer quickly and safely now.
